1
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
The question of whether or not we are alone in the universe has been answered.

2
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Independence Day is a action, adventure, science
fiction film directed by Roland Emmerich.

3
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Strange phenomena surface around the globe.

4
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:26,000
The skies ignite.

5
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Terror races through the world's major cities.

6
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:06,000
As these extraordinary events unfold, it becomes increasingly clear
that a force of incredible magnitude has arrived.

7
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:56,000
Its mission: total annihilation over the Fourth of July weekend.

8
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:46,000
The last hope to stop the destruction is an unlikely group of
people united by fate and unimaginable circumstances.

9
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:36,000
Capt. Steven Hiller, played by Will Smith.

10
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:26,000
President Thomas J. Whitmore, played by Bill Pullman.

11
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:16,000
David Levinson, played by Jeff Goldblum.

12
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:06,000
Marilyn Whitmore, played by Mary McDonnell.

13
01:02:50,000 --> 01:02:56,000
Julius Levinson, played by Judd Hirsch.

14
01:07:40,000 --> 01:07:46,000
General William Grey, played by Robert Loggia.

15
01:12:30,000 --> 01:12:36,000
Independence Day (also promoted as ID4) is a 1996 American science fiction action film directed by Roland Emmerich, written by Emmerich and the film's producer Dean Devlin, and
stars an ensemble cast that consists of Will Smith, Bill Pullman, Jeff Goldblum, Mary McDonnell, Judd Hirsch, Margaret Colin, Randy Quaid, Robert Loggia, Vivica A.

16
01:17:20,000 --> 01:17:26,000
Fox, James Rebhorn, and Harvey Fierstein.

17
01:22:10,000 --> 01:22:16,000
The film focuses on disparate groups of people who converge in the Nevada desert in the
aftermath of a worldwide attack by a powerful extraterrestrial race.

18
01:27:00,000 --> 01:27:06,000
With the other people of the world, they launch a counterattack
on July 4—Independence Day in the United States.

19
01:31:50,000 --> 01:31:56,000
While promoting Stargate in Europe, Emmerich conceived the film while
answering a question about his belief in the existence of alien life.

20
01:36:40,000 --> 01:36:46,000
Devlin and Emmerich decided to incorporate a large-scale attack having noticed that aliens in most
invasion films travel long distances in outer space only to remain hidden when reaching Earth.

21
01:41:30,000 --> 01:41:36,000
Shooting began on July 28, 1995, in New York City, and
the film was completed on October 8, 1995.

22
01:46:20,000 --> 01:46:26,000
Considered a significant turning point in the history of the Hollywood blockbuster, Independence Day was at
the forefront of the large-scale disaster film and sci-fi resurgence of the mid-late 1990s.

23
01:51:10,000 --> 01:51:16,000
It was released worldwide on July 3, 1996, but began showing on July 2 (the same day the film's story
begins) in original release as a result of a high level of anticipation among moviegoers.

24
01:56:00,000 --> 01:56:06,000
The film received mixed reviews, with praise for the performances, musical
score and visual effects, but criticism for its characters.

25
02:00:50,000 --> 02:00:56,000
It grossed over $817.4 million worldwide, becoming the highest-grossing film of 1996 and the
second-highest-grossing film ever at the time, behind Jurassic Park (1993).

26
02:05:40,000 --> 02:05:46,000
The film won the Academy Award for Best Visual Effects and was
nominated for the Academy Award for Best Sound.

27
02:10:30,000 --> 02:10:36,000
The sequel, Independence Day: Resurgence, was released 20 years later
on June 24, 2016, as part of a planned series of films.

28
02:15:20,000 --> 02:15:26,000
The film explores themes of spacecraft, showdown,
independence, patriotism, countdown.

29
02:20:10,000 --> 02:20:16,000
The tone is thought_provoking and intense.
